# kmeans (Rodinia)

K-means clustering.

## Benchmark source

Rodinia Benchmark Suite 3.1 (http://lava.cs.virginia.edu/wiki/rodinia).

Contact (for the original code):

- Ke Wang: kw5na@virginia.edu
- Shuai Che: sc5nf@cs.virginia.edu
- Kevin Skadron: skadron@cs.virginia.edu

## Build

```bash
make
```

`make` builds `kmeans_baseline.exe`, `kmeans_pim.exe`, `kmeans_pim_fast.exe`.

| Executable | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `kmeans_baseline.exe` | CPU baseline |
| `kmeans_pim.exe` | PUD-instrumented version; also executes every bbop on the CPU |
| `kmeans_pim_fast.exe` | PUD-instrumented version without CPU execution of bbops (used by `scripts/run_workloads.sh`) |

The compiler can be overridden, e.g., `make CC=gcc-15`. With Apple Clang,
OpenMP requires `libomp`.

## Input

The paper input has 10,000 objects with 16,384 integer features each. It is
generated by the included Rodinia generator (seed 11, the default):

```bash
make datagen.exe
./datagen.exe 10000 16384 11   # writes 10000_16384.txt (~558 MB)
```

`scripts/generate_kmeans_input.sh` runs the same steps and verifies
the checksum of the result.

## Run

```
kmeans_<version>.exe -i filename
    -i filename        : file containing data to be clustered
    -b                 : input file is in binary format
    -k                 : number of clusters (default is 5)
    -t threshold       : threshold value
    -n no. of threads  : number of threads
```

The paper uses:

```bash
./kmeans_pim_fast.exe -n 1 -i 10000_16384.txt
```

## Output

The PUD versions write `bbop_statistics.csv`, `transposition.csv`, and
`bbop_resolution.csv` to the working directory. See `../README.md` for their
format.
